WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.220
The veil has been lifted on Val! The mysterious&nbsp;
MCU figure gets grounded in "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.240
Wakanda Forever" — here's how the sequel&nbsp;
helps set up a new squad of anti-heroes.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.620
Phase Four of the Marvel Cinematic Universe has&nbsp;
largely occupied itself with reshuffling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.720
pack. Many of the heroes who survived the Infinity&nbsp;
Saga became respected elder superhero statesmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.800
and new heroes and legacy characters like&nbsp;
Yelena Belova and Kate Bishop emerged.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.800
Among the more interesting Phase Four arrivals is&nbsp;
Contessa Valentina Allegra de Fontaine , aka Val.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.120
"Don't call me that."

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:30.900
"Copy."

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.800
The Contessa is a mysterious figure&nbsp;
who's waltzed across various movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.440
and Disney+ shows like Nick Fury back in the day.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.220
Kevin Feige himself confirmed early&nbsp;
on that Val's various appearances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.640
are indeed building toward something,&nbsp;
and that something has since turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.120
out to be the new superhero team in the&nbsp;
MCU ... provided that you want to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.660
the antagonist-filled Thunderbolts lineup&nbsp;
"heroes" in the classic sense of the word.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.160
The main difference between Nick Fury and the&nbsp;
Contessa is, of course, that everyone knows who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.300
Fury is, while Val has gone through some pains&nbsp;
to keep everything but her full name and title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.100
of nobility hidden from the viewer. Affable as&nbsp;
she may seem, she's still clearly tough as nails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.780
and just ominous enough that you can't be&nbsp;
quite sure whether she's a hero or a villain.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.320
"Black Panther: Wakanda Forever"&nbsp;
continues Val's patchwork storyline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.580
by finally revealing something concrete&nbsp;
about the mysterious Contessa. The viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.760
find out that she's the director&nbsp;
of the MCU's version of the CIA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.960
and the ex-wife of the previously most&nbsp;
prominent Agency character, Everett Ross.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.320
This explains a lot of Val's actions&nbsp;
over the course of Phase Four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.160
and how she has the sway and know-how to pull&nbsp;
her power moves over the various super-people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.720
we've seen so far. Most importantly, "Black&nbsp;
Panther: Wakanda Forever" finally reveals in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.020
no uncertain terms just how Val will&nbsp;
be able to start the Thunderbolts.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:48.300
"We're not going to need a Captain&nbsp;
America. We're going to need a U.S. Agent."

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.940
"Wakanda Forever" makes clear&nbsp;
that unlike the high-tech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.680
superpowered world of Nick Fury's SHIELD,&nbsp;
the CIA is a far more mundane and secretive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.600
spookshow — and its director reflects this.&nbsp;
While Fury is basically a superhero himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.580
Val seems to prefer classic behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
espionage methods and deceptively easy banter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.260
that hides her true intentions. She's so good&nbsp;
at this that she runs rings around Everett Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.120
who not only knows Val very well, but is&nbsp;
also a highly capable CIA operative himself.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.220
Val's connections and position as the CIA director&nbsp;
put her in the perfect place to seek out and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.140
together a team like the Thunderbolts. Depending&nbsp;
on how you want to view it, she definitely seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.880
practical or amoral enough to tinker with a lineup&nbsp;
that consists of antagonists and gray-area players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.860
like The Winter Soldier, Yelena Belova, U.S.&nbsp;
Agent, Red Guardian, Ghost, and Taskmaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.660
With the Agency's assets at her disposal, she&nbsp;
also has little problem tracking them down.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:43.860
As for who may very well appear in the&nbsp;
"Thunderbolts" movie, Thaddeus "Thunderbolt" Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.240
Val has connections to him as well. Even if&nbsp;
the high-ranking general and Secretary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.380
State would somehow have managed to avoid the&nbsp;
director of the CIA in a professional context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.760
the fact that Everett and Thaddeus have both been&nbsp;
affiliated with the Joint Counter Terrorist Centre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.180
pretty much guarantees that Val and Thunderbolt&nbsp;
have ran into each other at some point.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.480
It's hard to think of a better position&nbsp;
for Val than the head of the CIA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.200
and now that "Black Panther: Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever" lets the cat out of the bag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.100
it'll be interesting to see how she wields&nbsp;
her considerable power. Hey, she could even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.410
turn out to be a Skrull or Madame Hydra, like in&nbsp;
the comics. How surprised would Everett Ross be?

